---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { sourceUrl, url } from "../../lib/urls";

const lifecycle = `import '@shanmukh98/lineframe-ui/styles.css';
import { initTheme, initToc } from '@shanmukh98/lineframe-ui';

// Call this in the browser, after the page markup exists.
export function mountLineframe() {
  const theme = initTheme();
  const destroyToc = initToc();

  return () => {
    destroyToc();
    theme.destroy();
  };
}`;
const themeMethods = [
  ["current()", "'light' | 'dark'", "Read the effective theme, including the system preference."],
  ["set('light' | 'dark')", "void", "Apply and persist an explicit choice."],
  ["reset()", "void", "Remove the saved override and follow the system again."],
  ["destroy()", "void", "Remove this controller’s listeners and release its initialization state."],
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="JavaScript APIs"
  description="Two optional behaviors. Explicit initialization, a small typed interface, and cleanup you can connect to your application lifecycle."
  section="Build with Lineframe"
  source="website/src/pages/docs/javascript.astro"
>
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <h2 id="choose-an-entry">Choose one initialization path</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Plain HTML:</strong> the version-pinned <code>lineframe.js</code>
        browser script automatically initializes theme preference and article navigation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A bundled application:</strong> import <code>initTheme</code> and
        <code>initToc</code>, then initialize them in your browser lifecycle.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An existing installation:</strong> separate
        <code>lineframe-theme.js</code> and <code>lineframe-toc.js</code>
        browser entries remain supported.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Do not mix auto-initialization scripts and explicit module setup without a deliberate
      ownership model. Idempotent initialization prevents duplicate listeners, but it is not a
      reason to load the same library twice.
    </p>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock id="js-lifecycle" code={lifecycle} language="ts" label="Framework-neutral lifecycle" />
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <p>
      The module has no import-time browser side effects. You may import it during server rendering,
      but the initialization functions require a browser. Call the returned teardown function when
      the owning view is removed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Theme preference is shared across the document. Give it one application-level owner rather
      than destroying the shared controller whenever an individual button unmounts. Repeated
      initialization is idempotent, not reference-counted.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="theme-api">Theme controller</h2>
      <p>
        <code>initTheme()</code> returns a controller. It follows the system until a person chooses a
        theme, keeps controls in sync, and persists that choice when browser storage is available.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="lf-table-wrap docs-reference-table"
      role="region"
      aria-label="Theme controller methods"
      tabindex="0"
    >
      <table class="lf-table">
        <caption>The theme API</caption>
        <thead
          ><tr
            ><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">Returns</th><th scope="col">Behavior</th
            ></tr
          ></thead
        >
        <tbody>
          {
            themeMethods.map(([method, returns, behavior]) => (
              <tr>
                <>
                  <th scope="row">
                    <code>{method}</code>
                  </th>
                  <td>
                    <code>{returns}</code>
                  </td>
                  <td>{behavior}</td>
                </>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <CodeBlock
      id="js-theme"
      language="ts"
      label="Explicit theme changes"
      code={`const theme = initTheme();

console.log(theme.current()); // 'light' or 'dark'
theme.set('dark');            // Persist an explicit choice.
theme.reset();                // Follow the system again.

// When the owning application is torn down:
theme.destroy();`}
    />
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <p>
        <code>set()</code> accepts only <code>'light'</code> or <code>'dark'</code>; use <code
          >reset()</code
        >, not <code>set('system')</code>, to follow the system. The saved preference uses the <code
          >lineframe-theme</code
        > storage key.
      </p>
      <p>
        The controller owns root theme state. Select accent palettes with
        <code>data-lineframe-accent</code> on a page or component; do not manually compete with root <code
          >data-theme</code
        > updates.
      </p>
      <h3 id="browser-global">The compatible browser global</h3>
      <p>
        The CDN entry preserves <code>window.LineframeTheme</code> with the same four methods. Use it
        after the theme script has loaded.
      </p>
    </div>
    <CodeBlock
      id="js-global"
      language="js"
      label="Browser-script consumers"
      code={`const theme = window.LineframeTheme;
if (theme) {
  console.log(theme.current());
  // Call from a real application action when appropriate:
  // theme.set('dark');
  // theme.reset();
}`}
    />
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <p>
        For standard controls, use the existing
        <a href={url("docs/tokens/#theme-preference")}>toggle and reset data attributes</a>
        instead of adding a second click handler.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="toc-api">Article table of contents</h2>
      <p>
        <code>initToc()</code> initializes matching article navigations already in the document and returns
        a cleanup function. It uses native anchors, keyboard-operable disclosure controls, and a current-section
        state. There is no continuously running DOM scan.
      </p>
    </div>
    <CodeBlock
      id="js-toc"
      language="ts"
      label="Initialize after rendering the article"
      code={`import { initToc } from '@shanmukh98/lineframe-ui';

const destroyToc = initToc();

// Before removing or replacing the article:
destroyToc();

// After rendering a replacement article, initialize again.
// const destroyNextToc = initToc();`}
    />
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <p>
        The required structure is documented in the
        <a href={url("docs/components/navigation/#article-toc")}>live navigation example</a>. Each
        TOC belongs inside its <code>data-lineframe-article</code> and needs a <code
          >data-lineframe-toc-list</code
        >. The article’s
        <code>data-lineframe-prose</code> supplies its <code>h2</code> and
        <code>h3</code> headings.
      </p>
      <p>
        Teardown removes listeners, cancels pending updates, and restores controller-managed markup
        and state. Reinitialize after you replace the article content. Prefer your own stable,
        unique heading IDs when links need to survive later edits.
      </p>
      <h3 id="progressive-enhancement">Progressive enhancement is the fallback</h3>
      <p>
        With JavaScript disabled, the prose still exists, native disclosures still work, and
        ordinary links still navigate. Keep an initially hidden TOC so an empty navigation does not
        occupy the page before initialization.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the focused implementations in
        <a href={sourceUrl("src/behaviors/")}>src/behaviors/</a>, or start with the
        <a href={url("docs/contributing/")}>contributor guide</a> if you find an edge case.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
